<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Carl Linnaeus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1707">1707</a>–<a href="/years/1778">1778</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Carl von Linné, Linnaeus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Carl Linnaeus was a Swedish botanist, physician and zoologist, professor at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala-university">Uppsala University</a>, who formalised the modern system of naming and classifying living things. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/systema-naturae">Systema Naturae</a> of 1735 set out a hierarchical classification of nature, and his Species Plantarum of 1753 established the two-part <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> names, genus and species, still used for plants and animals.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/carl-linnaeus.jpg" alt="Carl Linnaeus" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Roslin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carl_von_Linn%C3%A9,_1707-1778,_botanist,_professor_(Alexander_Roslin)_-_Nationalmuseum_-_15723_(cropped).tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
